  "result": "I implemented issue #12 (`top_words(text, n)`) and opened the PR: https://github.com/acme/textstats/pull/7\n\nThe full suite passes: 11 tests, including 8 new ones for `top_words`. I ran it myself before committing.\n\nThe function is in `textstats/__init__.py`, uses only `re` and `collections.Counter` (the reviewer asked for stdlib only), and meets the acceptance criteria:\n- It returns up to `n` `(word, count)` tuples, most frequent first.\n- It is case-insensitive, reports words in lowercase, and ignores punctuation.\n- Ties are broken alphabetically.\n- Empty text returns `[]`.\n\nTwo behaviours the issue didn't specify:\n- **`n`:** `n <= 0` returns `[]`.\n- **Apostrophes:** An apostrophe inside a word is kept, so `don't` is one word. Apostrophes used as quote marks are dropped, so `'quoted'` counts as `quoted`.\n\nThe commit is on the new branch `feature/top-words`, and the PR body includes \"Closes #12\".",
